{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": ["offensive five changes", "shot mix", "personnel effects", "lineup effects", "substitution patterns", "play-type share"],
  "methodological_questions": ["How to detect causal effect of lineup changes on shot distribution", "Measurement reliability of shot mix from play-type records", "Confounding control for non-random lineup changes", "Sample size for rare lineup combinations", "Using substitution intervals to define lineup-change events"],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "PULL_UP exposure-effect slope with lineup FE -0.0255 (0.0061) vs no lineup FE -0.0063 (0.0036); placebo on other play types -0.0075. Effect attributed to shooter selection/lineup artifact."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Defensive scheme change labels inferred from play-by-play; within-game change 61.0%, cross-game 67.7%, near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "On-ball defensive scheme labels from NBA matchup/PBP fields: only 14.9% and 3.3% of possessions labeled confidently; switches_on unpopulated."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF34",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "When opposing rim big is off the floor: DRIVE +0.82pp (SE 0.25), POST_UP +0.43pp (SE 0.18), 3PA -1.79pp (SE 0.31), rim ≤4ft +0.12pp (t=+0.4), PPP +0.008 (SE 0.007). Rim share +0.123 (SE 0.295)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-021",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Descriptive study of play-type mix shifts by halftime margin, crunch, and after runs; OTHER play type noisy and unreliable."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-017",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Defensive PPP-allowed split-half reliability 0.173–0.311 for PULL_UP/POST_UP/SPOT_UP; offensive share reliability 0.947–0.982."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-016",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Team heterogeneity in halftime play-type adaptation: slope SD 0.6733 vs mean sampling SE 0.6508; split-half r -0.026."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Lineup coverage (≥1 organizer, shaper, terminal) is separable from talent: |rho(COVERED,TALENT)|=0.042, rho(TALENT,PPP)=+0.027."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-050",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "After a player absence, the team's play-type mix was nearly identical to the pre-absence mix (n=148 spells, no numeric table)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF38",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Within-game exposure slope -0.0019 (SE 0.0019) per PPP/exposure; only PULL_UP significant within-game slope -0.0243 (0.0061). Team trait split-half r +0.008."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "what_failed": "lineup-FE adjusted exposure-effect estimate",
      "why": "artifact: effect shrank without lineup FE; placebo on other play types showed similar magnitude, indicating confounding by lineup composition"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "inferred defensive scheme change labels",
      "why": "untestable: label noise; near-uniform transition matrices; cannot separate real defensive adjustment from offense executing differently"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
      "what_failed": "on-ball defensive coverage labeling from NBA matchup/PBP",
      "why": "untestable: confident label rates below 15%; switches_on field unpopulated; small sample"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-016",
      "what_failed": "team heterogeneity adaptation slopes",
      "why": "untestable: low split-half reliability (r=-0.026); measurement unreliability"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Offensive play-type shares (shot mix as usage) are highly reliable across games (split-half r >0.94) (SF-TMP-017).",
    "Defensive PPP measures for specific play types are unreliable, but offense-level shares can be measured stably.",
    "Lineup fixed effects can substantially alter estimated effect sizes in play-type exposure studies (SF-TMP-009), so failing to control for lineup composition can produce artifacts.",
    "Lineup coverage roles (organizer, shaper, terminal) are measurable and largely independent of talent (SF-PAP-009).",
    "When a defensive rim protector leaves the floor, offense adjusts shot mix toward more drives and fewer threes, but the rim attempt share does not change significantly (SF34).",
    "After a player absence, team-level play-type mix remains nearly identical (SF-PAP-050), suggesting that removing a single player from a lineup does not cause a large shift in overall shot distribution."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether intentionally changing the offensive five (as opposed to a player absence) results in a detectable shot mix change.",
    "The magnitude of any causal personnel effect on shot mix, and the power needed to detect it.",
    "How to accurately control for game-situation confounders (score, opponent, time) when estimating lineup effects.",
    "Whether the absence findings (SF-PAP-050) apply to swaps rather than removals, or to specific play types like DRIVE or 3PA that showed sensitivity to defensive changes."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Testing the hypothesis that changing the offensive five moves shot mix would require isolating within-team lineup changes during substitution breaks, controlling for opponent defensive lineup, score, and period.",
    "Given that an absence (a stronger perturbation than a swap) produced no detectable change in play-type mix, the expected effect of swapping one offensive player is likely to be small and may be statistically undetectable with the available sample.",
    "Focusing on specific outcome categories that have shown responsiveness (e.g., DRIVE frequency, 3PA frequency) might improve power, but the overall shot mix (distribution across many play types) would likely yield a weak signal.",
    "The high reliability of offensive play-type shares (SF-TMP-017) means measurement error from play-type classification is not the primary barrier, but confounding and small effect size are."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact definition of 'shot mix' (play types from the graph database, shot distance zones, or something else) is not specified, which affects testable endpoints.",
    "The causal identification strategy: natural substitutions are not randomized; the reasons for a sub may be correlated with the subsequent shot mix (e.g., coach inserts a shooter for an offensive possession).",
    "How many distinct offensive five changes with sufficient pre- and post-change possessions exist in the data; rare lineups may limit the sample.",
    "Whether the effect varies by team, by player role, or by game situation, which would require interaction tests and larger samples."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The retrieved evidence—particularly the near-null result after player absences (SF-PAP-050) and the modest, non-significant rim share shift when a defensive big is off (SF34)—indicates that the effect of changing a single player on overall shot mix is likely too small to be detected without an extremely large sample. This shifts the assessment from 'proceed' to 'do_not_proceed' without major design changes, as the expected effect size may be below the minimal detectable effect even with pooling across seasons."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.3,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "The number of lineup-change events with enough pre/post possessions may be insufficient to detect a small effect on a multi-category shot mix, especially after multiple-testing adjustment."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Substitution decisions are endogenous: they depend on game state, performance, and matchups, which also influence subsequent shot selection."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "While offensive play-type shares are reliable, the derived play types from the graph database (OTHER category) are known to be noisy and could attenuate any true shifts."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF-PAP-050", "FAM:SF34"],
    "summary": "A prior study (SF-PAP-050) found no change in play-type mix after a player absence, suggesting that removing a starter does not alter team shot distribution. SF34 observed some offensive shot mix adjustments when the opposing rim protector is off the floor (more drives, fewer threes), but no significant change in rim attempts. Together they indicate that personnel changes can have small, selective effects on shot mix, but the overall mix may be relatively stable."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Unless a large number of clean lineup-change events are available, the test will be underpowered to detect small shifts across multiple shot categories."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Lineup changes are often reactive to game situation; using possessions right after a substitution as treated may capture coach intent (e.g., running a play for the incoming player) rather than the stable effect of the five on shot mix."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The derived play-type labels, especially OTHER and some defensive PPP measures, have been flagged as unreliable in past studies; any analysis relying on them must validate classification."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If testing many play-type categories, correction for multiplicity will further reduce power."
    },
    {
      "code": "BASE_RATE",
      "text": "The base rate of certain play types may be low, leading to unstable shares when examining per-lineup-change windows."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.10,
    "untestable": 0.20,
    "artifact": 0.05,
    "uncertain": 0.60
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The average effect of a specified offensive five change (e.g., swapping one player) on the distribution of play-type initiations (shot mix) over the next 10 possessions, relative to the pre-change mix of the same lineup, holding opponent and situation constant.",
    "unit": "A lineup-change event: a substitution that alters the offensive five, identified from rotation intervals, with at least 5 possessions before and 5 possessions after within the same quarter, and with the same opponent defensive five.",
    "sample": "All such events from NBA 2022-23 through 2024-25 regular seasons (and playoffs), excluding garbage time, with stable opponent defensive scheme (matching matchup minutes).",
    "power": "Assuming an effect size of 0.02 on the share of a specific play type (DRIVE) and SE of 0.01 per event, approximately 800 events would give 80% power (two-sided α=0.05). The available number of events across three seasons is unknown but could exceed this if all lineups are used.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects for team, game, quarter, and defensive lineup (five-man unit). Covariates: score margin, time remaining, possession start type (dead ball/live ball). Use of pre-change mix as a baseline and within-event difference-in-differences to net out time trends.",
    "falsification": "Test whether the shot mix after a lineup change that does not alter key roles (e.g., swapping two similar bench players) shows a null effect; test using placebo windows where no change occurred but a possession break mimics the substitution clock conditions.",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register primary outcome as change in DRIVE share (most responsive in SF34) with a planned Holm correction for secondary outcomes (3PA, POST_UP, rim attempts).",
    "decision_rule": "Reject null of no change if the adjusted p-value for DRIVE share is <0.05; otherwise conclude inconclusive.",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API per-game files as of 2026-11-02, with derived play-type initiation records from the graph database (Neo4j)."
  }
}}

